<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- No PackageReference at all, deliberately - and here the omission costs more argument than it did for
       DigitalOcean, so it is worth spelling out. Microsoft *does* publish a first-party, maintained SDK
       (Azure.ResourceManager.Compute / .Network / .Resources plus Azure.Identity), which is the obvious
       thing to reach for. It is declined on purpose:

         * It is not one dependency. The compute/network/resources trio plus Azure.Identity drags in
           Azure.Core, System.ClientModel, Microsoft.Identity.Client, Microsoft.IdentityModel.Abstractions,
           System.Memory.Data and their transitive closure. This repo pins and hand-justifies every package
           in Directory.Packages.props, and a closure that size is not something a reviewer can justify per
           entry.
         * Azure.Identity in particular is an ambient-credential discovery chain (environment variables,
           managed identity, shared token cache, Azure CLI, Visual Studio). Servyx's whole secret discipline
           is that credentials come from ISecretStore by URN and from nowhere else; a library whose default
           behaviour is to go looking for credentials in the environment is the opposite of that rule, and
           suppressing it would mean configuring away most of what the library is for.
         * The surface actually needed is small: one OAuth2 token exchange and five ARM verbs
           (PUT/GET/DELETE a resource, list resources by tag). That is well under the cost of the closure.

       So this adapter hand-rolls ARM REST over System.Net.Http / System.Net.Http.Json / System.Text.Json,
       all three of which ship in the net10.0 shared framework - "no new dependency" here is a fact about the
       build, not an aspiration. Note the sibling infra projects reference
       Microsoft.Extensions.{DependencyInjection,Logging}.Abstractions for their DI registration helpers;
       this project has neither, which is also why it can carry no logger and therefore no code path that
       could log the Azure client secret or an access token. -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Depends on Servyx.Domain directly, not Servyx.Application, for the same reason
         Servyx.Infrastructure.Docker, .Ssh and .DigitalOcean do: Application's purpose is to consume
         IProvisioner/ITransport via DI, so a reference the other way would create a cycle. It also
         references no other infrastructure project - in particular not Servyx.Infrastructure.Ssh, even
         though every TargetDescriptor it produces is consumed by the SSH transport, and not
         Servyx.Infrastructure.DigitalOcean, even though this adapter is the second instance of the same
         shape. That is the layering rule and it is load-bearing here: if this project could see the SSH
         adapter, "shape I contains no install logic" would be a matter of discipline rather than a matter
         of what is reachable. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Servyx.Domain\Servyx.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
